d":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Address (or explicitly scope out, with rationale) the legacy-blob tip case where `_object_type`'s `cat-file -t <blob-oid>` can lazily fetch in a partial clone while pinned to the 5s local budget — otherwise narrow the 'any partial clone benefits' claim to the commit-tip shape.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.4,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: `_object_type` (`cat-file -t` on the commit, which blob:none clones carry) stays on the local budget.","Plan: 'This is portable (no CI-specific step; any partial clone benefits)'.","_read_blob_bytes handles obj_type == 'blob' where `oid` is the blob itself; `_object_type` at line 355 runs `cat-file -t oid` on that blob oid at `_LOCAL_TIMEOUT_SECS`.","In a blobless/partial clone a bare legacy blob object is not local, so `cat-file -t <blob-oid>` is itself a lazy-fetchable op, unlike `cat-file -t` on a commit (which the clone carries)."],"finding":"For a legacy-blob ref tip in a partial clone, `_object_type` (`cat-file -t <blob-oid>`, kept on the 5s local budget) can itself trigger a lazy promisor fetch of the absent blob and time out, so the plan's broadened 'any partial clone benefits' claim is not fully met for the legacy path.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f69052b98b1c115c6","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach paragraph / AC line 2 (`_object_type` still passes `_LOCAL_TIMEOUT_SECS`)","norm_id":"nd6d056797bdef5f8","priority":1.0,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A rebar <=0.13 git-remote ref tip that is a bare lock.json blob is read inside a blob:none clone whose blob object was never fetched."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfi